#                                            timestamps)
#   Tunnel-up -> viable:                    8.889s
#   VERDICT: REKEY_TIMEOUT-bound (same pattern).
#
# --- OVERALL VERDICT ---
# All 4 sampled cycles (1 smoke test + 3 timed) show the SAME pattern,
# clustering extremely tightly:
#   - Two handshake_initiation packets go out back-to-back when the tunnel
#     enters "connecting tunnel" (2-hop WG: one to entry, one to exit).
#   - Only ONE handshake_response comes back on the first attempt (entry hop).
#   - The exit hop's handshake does NOT complete until gotatun's
#     HANDSHAKE(REKEY_TIMEOUT) fires and forces a fresh handshake_initiation,
#     at a strikingly consistent +5.020s / +5.021s / +5.020s / +5.023s after
#     the FIRST handshake_initiation (WireGuard's standard 5s REKEY_TIMEOUT
#     constant, not randomly distributed — 100% reproduction, 4/4 cycles).
#   - The connection_monitor's probe only ever succeeds AT OR AFTER that
#     post-rekey handshake_response (within 0.2-1.1s), never before it, even
#     though probes are already firing on a ~3s cadence well before the
#     rekey lands (initial probe ~80-180ms after tunnel-up, one retry at
#     +3s that always still fails).
#   - This is REKEY_TIMEOUT-bound, NOT cadence-bound. The originally
#     "expected" finding (handshake completing well before the successful
#     probe, i.e. we're just waiting out the probe's own cadence timer) does
#     NOT hold here: the exit-hop handshake genuinely isn't usable until the
#     forced rekey retry, ~5s in.
#   - Implication for the planned fix (gate probing on exit WG handshake
#     rather than on probe cadence): gating on "handshake initiated" won't
#     help, since the first initiation reliably fails to elicit an exit-hop
#     response. Gating on "handshake_response received" (the SECOND one) is
#     the correct target — but note the ~5s REKEY_TIMEOUT wait itself is an
#     inherent floor this specific fix does NOT remove; it will only remove
#     the cadence-driven idle gaps layered around it (e.g. cycle 2's extra
#     probe-cadence tick, and possibly some of the ~2.5-3.6s tail between
#     probe success and the "Connected" state flip, if that tail is itself
#     probe-interval-driven). Worth flagging to whoever designs the
#     handshake-gated probing change: the root cause of the exit hop's FIRST
#     handshake attempt failing (not just "nobody's listening yet, so gate on
#     handshake") may itself be worth a follow-up investigation, since fixing
#     that would remove the ~5s REKEY_TIMEOUT wait entirely rather than just
#     avoiding redundant cadence delay around it.
#
# Per-cycle totals (shell `time`, official 3-cycle run only):
#   Cycle 1: real 0m 10.40s   (tunnel-up->viable 7.891s)
#   Cycle 2: real 0m 12.34s   (tunnel-up->viable 9.547s)
#   Cycle 3: real 0m 11.62s   (tunnel-up->viable 8.889s)
#   Mean:    real ~11.45s     (mean tunnel-up->viable ~8.78s)
# Matches the ~11s total / ~8.7s viability-gate baseline cited in the task
# background.
# ============================================================================
